You previously wrote a pytest file against this contract:

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass, field

@dataclass
class Section:
    title: str
    level: int
    own_text: str
    children: list["Section"] = field(default_factory=list)

def parse_sections(text: str) -> Section:
    """Parse a full Markdown document string into a Section tree."""
```

Coverage analysis on the (hidden to you) implementation shows one branch your
tests never exercise: the case where a heading closes one or more previously
open headings of the same or deeper level, i.e. two headings at the SAME
level must become siblings under the document root (or under the same
parent), not nested one under the other.

Write exactly one additional pytest test, self-contained (inline Markdown
string, no fixtures), named `test_two_headings_at_the_same_level_are_siblings`,
that:

- Parses a document with two top-level `#` headings, "One" then "Two", each
  with distinct body text.
- Asserts both end up as direct children of the document root (both appear
  in `root.children`, and neither is a child of the other).
- Asserts "One"'s own_text does NOT contain "Two"'s body text and vice versa.

Return exactly one fenced Python code block, nothing else — no prose before
or after it, no import line (the test will be appended to an existing file
that already imports `parse_sections`).
